WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.224
hello-devops runs as a Deployment with three replicas.

2
00:00:06.224 --> 00:00:08.900
The Deployment does not make Pods itself.

3
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:15.967
It owns a ReplicaSet, and the ReplicaSet keeps three Pods of version 1.0.0 running.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.983
In front of them, a Service sends requests to every ready Pod.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.843
Now you change the image to 1.1.0.

6
00:00:23.843 --> 00:00:29.733
That line sits under template, and only a change to the template starts a rollout.

7
00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:31.998
Changing replicas would not.

8
00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.521
That is only scaling.

9
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:41.100
On its next pass, the Deployment controller compares its template with the template inside each ReplicaSet it owns.

10
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:44.470
1.1.0 is not 1.0.0.

11
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.616
None matches.

12
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:51.597
So it creates a new ReplicaSet for the new template, starting at zero Pods.

13
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.633
Two settings set the pace.

14
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:57.509
maxSurge is one: at most one extra Pod, so four in all.

15
00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:01.857
maxUnavailable is zero: never fewer than three ready Pods.

16
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:03.427
Step one.

17
00:01:03.427 --> 00:01:05.850
The new ReplicaSet scales to one.

18
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.080
Now there are four Pods: three old, one new.

19
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.284
The new Pod is starting, but it is not ready yet.

20
00:01:12.284 --> 00:01:14.049
The Service sends it nothing.

21
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:17.960
And with maxUnavailable at zero, no old Pod may go.

22
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.699
So the Deployment waits.

23
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.580
Readiness decides when each step may happen.

24
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:27.209
The kubelet checks the new Pod, and writes the result into the Pod's status.

25
00:01:27.433 --> 00:01:31.096
If it never became ready, the rollout would stall right here.

26
00:01:31.096 --> 00:01:33.377
The three old Pods would keep serving.

27
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.434
Kubernetes would not roll back on its own.

28
00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:37.581
It waits for you.

29
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.528
But this one passes.

30
00:01:39.528 --> 00:01:40.565
It is ready.

31
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.875
Only now may an old Pod go.

32
00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:45.452
The old ReplicaSet scales to two.

33
00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:47.161
Look at the Service now.

34
00:01:47.161 --> 00:01:50.212
It sends requests to ready Pods of both versions.

35
00:01:50.212 --> 00:01:53.388
So the new version must work alongside the old one.

36
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:55.770
Step three.

37
00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:00.603
The new ReplicaSet scales to two, and waits for that Pod to be ready.

38
00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:02.431
Step four.

39
00:02:02.431 --> 00:02:04.505
The old one scales to one.

40
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:06.425
The new one scales to three.

41
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:10.092
And finally, the old one scales to zero.

42
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.078
At no point were there fewer than three ready Pods.

43
00:02:15.078 --> 00:02:20.900
The price is room: one extra Pod at a time needs spare CPU and memory on some node.

44
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:23.593
The old ReplicaSet is not deleted.

45
00:02:23.593 --> 00:02:26.992
It stays, at zero, as a record of that version.

46
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.809
That record is what makes rollback fast.

47
00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:32.744
Say version 1.1.0 has a bug.

48
00:02:32.744 --> 00:02:35.551
You run kubectl rollout undo.

49
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:39.229
The undo copies the old template back into the Deployment.

50
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:44.240
Now the template matches the old ReplicaSet, the one still waiting at zero.

51
00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:50.895
So the Deployment scales it back up, and the new one down, by the same rules, one ready Pod at a time.

52
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.953
The undo is itself a rollout.

53
00:02:53.953 --> 00:02:57.596
The old version comes back with a new number, revision 3.

54
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.352
A rolling update is two ReplicaSets trading Pods.

55
00:03:01.352 --> 00:03:08.672
One goes up, one goes down, inside maxSurge and maxUnavailable, and every step waits for a ready Pod.
